course: dictionary
lesson: 10810
title: Vase: a container or an ornament
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A vase is a container, often tall and pretty, that holds flowers in water. When you meet a new word, read the words around it, because they are its context and they leave clues. «She put the tulips in the vase and filled it with water.» Tulips and water go inside something, so a vase must be the thing that holds them.
explain: Sometimes a vase has no flowers at all. «Imagine your friend knocks a vase off the teacher's desk, and the teacher asks the class who did it.» A vase can fall and break, so it is a solid object that stands on its own. Ask yourself what would fit, then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: A vase is a container, often tall and pretty, for holding flowers in water.
fact: A vase can also be an ornament that stands on its own, with nothing in it.
fact: «Vase» is a noun, and its plural is «vases».
fact: In art, a vase is often drawn in a still life, a picture of everyday objects.
fact: Context clues, like breaking or flowers, can help you work out what a word means.
exercise: «She put the tulips in the vase and filled it with water.» What is a vase? | A small table for the kitchen | A type of flower with bright petals | *A container for holding flowers in water | A tall glass window
exercise: «The vase on the shelf was just for show, with no flowers in it.» What is the vase used for here? | As a bird's nest | *As an ornament that stands on its own | As a pot for growing trees | As a bowl for soup
exercise: Which word is the plural of vase? | vace | vasis | vasen | *vases
test: «Imagine your friend knocks a vase off the teacher's desk.» What does the sentence tell you about a vase? | *It is a thing that can break if it falls | It is a soft cushion for sitting on | It is a kind of cake | It is a bird that can sing
test: «The children shared the flowers equally into the vases.» What does equally mean here? | Very quickly and without care | Loudly and with a big noise | Unhappily and slowly | *In the same amount for each one
exam: «Blaming the cat for a broken vase lets someone else take the blame.» What is a vase in this sentence? | A cup for drinking tea | A lamp that gives light | *A breakable container or ornament that someone can knock over | A soft pillow for sleeping
